- **No native Windows receipt exists yet.** Nothing here ran on Windows. `host_identity()` has not executed on any machine, native or Wine. The `host:`/`gl:` lines have never been seen in a real kotorphasor.log. Closing that needs W2: the user runs the installer and receipt script on a real Windows PC with a GPU.
- The PowerShell scripts were parsed and exercised with pwsh 7.3.12 on Linux. They were not run under Windows PowerShell 5.1. Registry and CIM calls in the receipt script need Windows, so the script has not been executed at all.
- The overlay-name match (`galaxy*`, `*overlay*`, `nvspcap*`) is checked as source text only. It has not been observed against a live module list.
- The GOG K1 Bink and the K2 Bink shas are unrecorded. K2, and K1 GOG if its Bink differs from Steam's, needs `-TrustBinkSha256` until someone hashes the intact GOG `binkw32.dll`.
- The `CMakeLists.txt`/ctest registration of the two Python tests is not done, because CMake is forbidden. They run by hand as shown above.
# Install on Windows (PowerShell)

This page covers the two PowerShell scripts for a native Windows install:

- `scripts\install\windows\Install-KotorPhasor.ps1` installs or removes KotorPhasor.
- `scripts\receipts\windows-native-receipt.ps1` writes a receipt after one test run.

The Linux and Wine flow is in [install.md](install.md).

## Let PowerShell run the scripts

Windows blocks unsigned scripts by default. It also marks downloaded files as coming from the internet. Open PowerShell in the KotorPhasor folder and run:

```powershell
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass
Get-ChildItem -Recurse .\scripts, .\build | Unblock-File
```

`-Scope Process` lasts until you close that window. It does not change any system setting.

If the game is under `Program Files`, open PowerShell with "Run as administrator".

## Install, check, remove

```powershell
.\scripts\install\windows\Install-KotorPhasor.ps1 -GameDir 'C:\GOG Games\Star Wars - KotOR' -SourceDir .\build
.\scripts\install\windows\Install-KotorPhasor.ps1 -GameDir 'C:\GOG Games\Star Wars - KotOR' -Action Status
.\scripts\install\windows\Install-KotorPhasor.ps1 -GameDir 'C:\GOG Games\Star Wars - KotOR' -Action Uninstall
```

Install refuses a game exe or `binkw32.dll` it does not recognise. It copies only the address table for your build.

Uninstall reads `kotorphasor\install-manifest.json`. It refuses the whole manifest, and removes nothing, if any path in it:

- is rooted
- has a `.` or `..` part
- points outside the game folder
- names a file the installer never writes

It restores `binkw32.dll` from the backup. If the backup is gone, it uses `binkw32_real.dll`, but only when that file still matches the original. Your scripts and `kotorphasor.log` stay.

## Write a receipt

Start the game, reach the main menu, press F12 on the keyboard, then quit. Then run:

```powershell
.\scripts\receipts\windows-native-receipt.ps1 -GameDir 'C:\GOG Games\Star Wars - KotOR' -F12Result pass
```

Use `fail` if the console did not open. The receipt goes to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\KotorPhasor\receipts\` unless you pass `-OutFile`.

Check it, with the log, on any machine with Python:

```sh
python3 tools/platform_receipt_test.py --validate receipt.json --log kotorphasor.log
```

## What a receipt shows

A receipt is supporting evidence that a run was native Windows. It is not proof.

- It reads only the newest game start in `kotorphasor.log`. It needs exactly one host line and one GL line there, written by the runtime with that run's random id.
- Wine shows up through one runtime signal: an ntdll export only Wine has. Some patched Wine builds hide it. The receipt also checks for the `HKLM\Software\Wine` registry key, which only helps when the receipt script runs inside that same Wine prefix.
- GL names from Mesa, Linux drivers, macOS or VM adapters are refused. A Wine run on an NVIDIA driver reports the same GL names as Windows, so it can still pass.
- A Lua script can write to `kotorphasor.log` with Lua's `io` library. It can also add a line by printing text with a line break in it. Until the runtime blocks that, a script could fake these lines.

